 1547: 	 *  window.
 1548: 	 *
 1549: 	 *  *** ONE FIX DESIGN IS TRUE OF THE LATCH AND FALSE OF THE DECLINE. ***
 1550: 	 *  Above, `odata` is a local starting at 0, so declining means the tail
 1551: 	 *  returns 0 and a declined write does nothing.  HERE, odata is already
 1552: 	 *  loaded from the shadow register file before the switch, and the tail
 1553: 	 *  STORES a declined write into that file.  So 'decline' in this window
 1554: 	 *  means store-and-return, not discard -- which is what the five
 1555: 	 *  implemented cases already do, and is kept deliberately.
 1556: 	 *
 1557: 	 *  *** THAT NEARLY SHIPPED A WORSE DEFECT THAN THE ONE IT FIXES, AND A
 1558: 	 *  PASS-2 SEAT CAUGHT IT. ***  An earlier draft of this comment claimed
 1559: 	 *  "no declined offset can reach a load-bearing one".  THAT IS FALSE, and
 1560: 	 *  the reason is one character: the tail store indexes with TRUNCATING
 1561: 	 *  division, `dma_reg[relative_addr / sizeof(uint32_t)]`.
 1562: 	 *
 1563: 	 *  A byte access at +0x05 does not match `case 0x04` -- the switch tests
 1564: 	 *  the exact value -- so it reaches this arm, is declined and latched, and
 1565: 	 *  THEN the tail stores it into dma_reg[1].  The same alias exists at
 1566: 	 *  +0x09..0x0b -> dma_reg[2], +0x0d..0x0f -> dma_reg[3] and
 1567: 	 *  +0x15..0x17 -> dma_reg[5].
 1568: 	 *
 1569: 	 *  Those four ARE the transfer: `case 0x18` reads dma_reg[3] (count) and
 1570: 	 *  dma_reg[5] (enable) as its arming condition, then dma_reg[1] as the
 1571: 	 *  destination and dma_reg[2] as the length.  So a guest could arm and AIM
 1572: 	 *  a DMA transfer entirely through declined offsets, bypassing every check
 1573: 	 *  the implemented cases perform.  PRE-FIX each of those accesses exited
 1574: 	 *  instead, so this fix would have CREATED the capability -- the same shape
 1575: 	 *  as #460's Option C, which was rejected for exactly this reason.
 1576: 	 *
 1577: 	 *  THE RULE: an UNALIGNED access never stores.  Alignment alone separates
 1578: 	 *  "unimplemented word" from "misaligned access to an implemented one",
 1579: 	 *  and only the first keeps store-and-return.
 1580: 	 *
 1581: 	 *  WHY THAT IS SOUND, as a proof rather than an appeal to intuition about
 1582: 	 *  switch statements -- and INDEPENDENTLY RE-DERIVED by a review seat
 1583: 	 *  before it was believed.  On multiples of 4 the map off -> off/4 is
 1584: 	 *  INJECTIVE, so an aligned offset reaching this arm cannot share an index
 1585: 	 *  with any implemented case: if it did, it would BE that case and the
 1586: 	 *  switch would have matched it.  The implemented DMA offsets are
 1587: 	 *  0x04/0x08/0x0c/0x14/0x18, i.e. indices 1/2/3/5/6; every aligned offset
 1588: 	 *  reaching here yields 0, 4, or 7..31.  Disjoint by construction, not by
 1589: 	 *  inspection.  Truncation can only collide for UNALIGNED addresses, which
 1590: 	 *  is exactly what the guard rejects.
 1591: 	 *
 1592: 	 *  *** A FUTURE `case` AT AN ALIGNED OFFSET KEEPS THIS TRUE AUTOMATICALLY;
 1593: 	 *  ONE AT AN UNALIGNED OFFSET WOULD BREAK IT. ***  There is no such thing
 1594: 	 *  in this device, and the same seat checked the CONTROL window: its
 1595: 	 *  default arm has no store tail at all, so there is no aliasing channel
 1596: 	 *  there to guard, which is why the rule is stated only here.
 1597: 	 *
 1598: 	 *  The bitmap's own bounds guard does NOT help here -- it protects the
 1599: 	 *  latch array, and dma_reg is a different array reached by a different
 1600: 	 *  index.  Two arrays, two bounds problems, and only one of them was
 1601: 	 *  guarded before a seat read the tail.
